{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(0\\)","B":"\\(\\dfrac83\\)","C":"\\(4\\)","D":"\\(5\\)","E":"\\(\\dfrac{16}{3}\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1962_AMC_12_Q26","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_235"],"contentHash":"bc331620d665ad7686f0045d51fb8e2ec8dfd585b2c9014f937185886fd6fea6","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complete-square-bound","label":"Complete the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-square-bound":["math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"discriminant-bound","label":"Bound possible function values","methodDirectNodeIds":{"discriminant-bound":["cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_223"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"complete-square","label":"Write a constant minus a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-square":["cnm_235","math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_235","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_148"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_148"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":26,"question":"For any real value of \\(x\\) the maximum value of \\(8x-3x^2\\) is:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"A real square is nonnegative, so writing the expression as a constant minus a square will reveal its maximum. Completing the square gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x-3x^2\n&=-3\\left[\\left(x-\\frac43\\right)^2-\\frac{16}{9}\\right]\\\\\n&=\\frac{16}{3}-3\\left(x-\\frac43\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe subtracted term is nonnegative for every real \\(x\\), so the expression is at most \\(\\frac{16}{3}\\). Equality occurs when \\(x=\\frac43\\), making this bound attainable. The maximum is therefore \\(\\frac{16}{3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"presen